<Project>

    <!--
        Extra inputs to fold into a project's own hash.

        The CI tool reads a project's properties and items straight from MSBuild's evaluation
        API (see MsBuildProjectEvaluator), so nothing here has to report them: an item or
        property is queryable without running any target, which is why the CiProjectInfo target
        this file used to declare, and the build/project-info.proj that invoked it, are both gone.

        What the tool cannot infer is an input that changes a project's output without appearing
        among its sources, packages or build properties — a code generator reading a file the
        project does not list, say, or a value baked in from the environment. Declare those, and
        a change to one invalidates the project's cache entry the way a source edit would:

            <ItemGroup>
                <CiHashInput Include="schema-version=3"/>
            </ItemGroup>

        Anything not declared and not otherwise covered is, by definition, an input the cache
        cannot see — which is how a stale artifact gets served under a hash that still looks
        correct. When in doubt, declare it: an unnecessary entry costs one rebuild, a missing
        one costs a green build that tested nothing.
    -->

</Project>
